  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1248 PM CDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nCurrent Conditions/Tonight:\n\nGorgeous conditions across the Northland this afternoon with mostly \nsunny skies and highs in the 70s. Surface high pressure starts to \nshift to the east tonight with another dose of cool temperatures \nexpected. Low temperatures tonight will dip back into the 40s \nwith our colder spots likely falling into the upper 30s again. \n\nMonday:\n\nSurface high pressure will have been shunted off to the east with \nsoutherly warm air advection moving back into the Northland. High \ntemperatures will increase a couple degrees from the previous day \nwith some isolated spots seeing low 80s. Precipitation chances \nincrease late Monday and into Tuesday as a Low pressure system moves \nacross Canada. Some of the CAMs are quite bullish with the onset of \nprecipitation initially in the early evening hours as a weak \nshortwave rounds the upper level ridge in place ahead of the \napproaching system. For now, we have limited PoPs to 15% as \nsoundings still look fairly dry during this time period and the \nstrength of the available forcing does not look overly strong \nwith height rises still in play aloft. Better forcing for ascent\narrives later into the night as the system moves across the \nNorthern Plains with a cold front sliding into the Red River \nValley. The warm sector ahead of the cold front in our CWA will \nbe able to generate some instability with MUCAPE projected to be\naround 1000 J/kg. Bulk shear is pretty minimal through Tuesday \nmorning and mid level lapse rates are not overly favorable so \ncurrent thinking is that any storms that do fire off will remain\nsub severe producing small hail. The strongest storms look to \nremain to the west at this time where SPC has a marginal risk \noutlined. However, this could shift east into our region. Right \nnow the HRRR is the most aggressive of the CAMs with a linear \ncomplex marching across northern MN in the morning hours. Still \nhave a healthy amount of skepticism in this scenario with mean \nwind flow not overly strong and no sustainable boundary present \nin the models during that timeframe. \n\nTuesday:\n\nLingering showers and storms in the morning are possible, especially \nacross northern MN. Another round of showers and storms are expected \nto develop in the afternoon as the low pressure and associated \nfrontal boundaries cross the Northland. Instability recovery from \nthe morning activity will have to be monitored but the current \nexpectation is for the region to see MUCAPE bounce back to over 1000 \nJ/kg as surface temperates heat up into the upper 70s and 80s. Bulk \nshear will also see a slight uptick as mid level flow increases with \na small jet streak rounding the base of the trough. With these \ningredients in play we could see some isolated severe storms capable \nof producing 1\" hail and damaging winds. SPC has a marginal risk \n(level 1 out of 5) outlined for most of our region except near \nthe Brainerd Lakes area. Best timing for severe storms to\ninitiate still looks to be in the afternoon and evening hours.\n\nWednesday-Friday:\n\nLow pressure will be sliding into the Great Lakes on Wednesday with \nsome lingering showers and storms possible through the day. Any \nstorms that do manage to form would likely not be severe as the mid \nlevel flow will be weaker and not capable of sustaining strong \nupdrafts. Northerly flow will stream back into the region with highs \nback in the 70s through the end of the work week. The upper level \npattern maintains northwest flow with the 12Z suite of \ndeterministic guidance keeping activity out of the region as surface \nhigh pressure expands across Manitoba and through the Upper Midwest.",
